Object.assign(window.deepArticles, {
  "karafuto":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Karafuto",infoboxKicker:"All-Sakhalin region of integral Japan",
    portrait:{src:"assets/flags/japan.svg",alt:"Flag of the Empire of Japan",caption:"Karafuto uses the Japanese national flag; it has no separate international sovereignty"},
    lead:"Karafuto is the Japanese name and administrative region for Sakhalin, the long island north of Hokkaidō. The historical prefecture governed the island south of the fiftieth parallel from 1905; Japanese forces acquired the northern half as Soviet authority collapsed, making the entire island an integral region of Japan and a northern hinge between the home islands, the Sea of Okhotsk, and the Russian Far Eastern State.",
    canon:"Southern Karafuto's 1905–1943 history is historical. Japan's possession of all Sakhalin, integral status, Japanese nationality, strategic relation to the Russian Far East, and use within the inner yen area are established setting canon. The exact northern occupation calendar, final prefectural law, representation, census, district map, and names adopted for northern cities remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"Geography",html:"<p>Karafuto stretches between the Sea of Japan and the Sea of Okhotsk, separated from Hokkaidō by La Pérouse Strait and from the Asian mainland by the Strait of Tartary. Mountain chains, forests, wetlands, cold currents, ice, and long distances concentrate settlement along ports, rail corridors, mines, fishing grounds, and the more temperate south.</p>"},
      {id:"south",title:"Southern Karafuto before 1945",html:"<p>The Treaty of Portsmouth transferred Sakhalin south of the fiftieth parallel to Japan in 1905. The Karafuto Agency began in 1907, moved its capital from Ōtomari to <a href='toyohara.html'>Toyohara</a> in 1908, and developed railways, forestry, pulp, fisheries, coal, agriculture, and new Japanese towns. Karafuto became <em>naichi</em>, or inner land, in 1943.</p>"},
      {id:"north",title:"Acquisition of northern Sakhalin",html:"<p>Japan had occupied the north from 1920 to 1925 and retained oil and coal concessions afterward. When the Soviet state disintegrated during the German–Soviet war, Japanese naval and army authorities secured northern ports, fields, communications, and the approaches to the Okhotsk Sea. The <a href='northern-karafuto-integration.html'>integration settlement</a> extended Karafuto administration over the whole island.</p>"},
      {id:"government",title:"Government and nationality",html:"<p>Karafuto belongs legally to Japan proper rather than to the associated-state system. Residents possess or acquire Japanese nationality under regional law, use the domestic yen, and fall under Tokyo's strategic authority. Prefectural government, police, resource agencies, military commands, municipalities, and corporate administrations overlap more heavily than in an ordinary home-island prefecture.</p>"},
      {id:"people",title:"Population and cultural hierarchy",html:"<p>Japanese settlers, Koreans, inherited Russian communities, <a href='ainu-people.html'>Ainu</a>, <a href='nivkh-people.html'>Nivkh</a>, <a href='uilta-people.html'>Uilta</a>, and smaller northern populations inhabit the island. Formal nationality does not remove distinctions of language, ancestry, occupation, settlement history, or access to administration and corporate advancement.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Resource and transport economy",html:"<p><a href='karafuto-resource-industries.html'>Karafuto's economy</a> joins fisheries, timber, pulp and paper, coal, oil, natural gas, ports, railways, power, and cold-region engineering. It feeds the Japanese industrial system while also supporting the Okhotsk shipping and aviation network that serves the protected Russian Far East.</p>"},
      {id:"strategy",title:"Northern strategic position",html:"<p>Airfields, listening stations, ports, naval facilities, weather stations, and control of the straits make the island Japan's northern gate. Karafuto shields Hokkaidō, watches the Amur and Vladivostok approaches, and allows Tokyo to influence Russian Far Eastern transport without incorporating that protected state.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Karafuto in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Karafuto is presented domestically as an ordinary part of the Japanese national map, yet remains visibly a settlement and security frontier. Japanese dominates public life. Preservation institutions maintain selected Ainu, Nivkh, Uilta, Russian, and Korean records, but administrative recognition is stronger than linguistic recovery.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"toyohara.html",kicker:"Regional capital",label:"Toyohara"},{href:"maoka.html",kicker:"Western port",label:"Maoka"},{href:"northern-karafuto-integration.html",kicker:"Post-Soviet settlement",label:"Northern Karafuto Integration"},{href:"karafuto-resource-industries.html",kicker:"Political economy",label:"Karafuto Resource Industries"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Constitutional category",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"},{href:"japan.html",kicker:"Sovereign state",label:"Empire of Japan"}],
    facts:[["Japanese name","Karafuto"],["Geographic extent","All of Sakhalin"],["Historical southern acquisition","Treaty of Portsmouth · 1905"],["Karafuto Agency","Established 1907"],["Capital","Toyohara"],["Inner-land status","Southern prefecture from 1943; all-island region after Soviet collapse"],["Nationality","Japanese"],["Status in 1985","Integral northern region of Japan proper"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karafuto_Prefecture",label:"Historical reference — Karafuto Prefecture"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakhalin",label:"Geographical reference — Sakhalin"}]
  }),

  "toyohara":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Toyohara",category:"Cities of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Capital of Karafuto",
    lead:"Toyohara is the capital and principal administrative city of Karafuto. Established as the Russian settlement Vladimirovka and renamed after Japan acquired southern Sakhalin, it became the prefectural capital in 1908 and grew into the island's rail, government, education, communications, and service center.",
    canon:"The Russian foundation, Japanese renaming, 1908 capital status, planned-city form, rail role, and pre-1945 institutions are historical. Survival as the capital of an all-island Japanese Karafuto and its postwar administrative and technical expansion are extrapolated from fixed territorial canon; population, mayoral history, districts, and exact postwar institutions remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"foundation",title:"Vladimirovka and Japanese acquisition",html:"<p>Russian settlers founded Vladimirovka in the nineteenth century in the Susuya Valley. Japan acquired the southern district after the Russo-Japanese War, renamed the settlement Toyohara, and selected it for a planned inland capital protected from direct maritime attack.</p>"},
      {id:"capital",title:"Prefectural capital",html:"<p>Toyohara replaced Ōtomari as capital in 1908. The Karafuto Agency, courts, police, schools, railway administration, hospitals, press, banks, shrines, and commercial offices gave the city functions far beyond its size.</p>"},
      {id:"planning",title:"Planned urban form",html:"<p>A gridded street plan, government quarter, station district, warehouses, housing, schools, and civic buildings expressed Japanese settlement authority. Snow clearance, heating, drainage, fire control, timber construction, and later reinforced public buildings shaped an urban style adapted to the northern climate.</p>"},
      {id:"rail",title:"Railway and road center",html:"<p>Lines connect Toyohara with Ōtomari, <a href='maoka.html'>Maoka</a>, mining and forestry districts, and later northern Sakhalin. The city schedules freight, repairs rolling stock, assigns scarce locomotives, and transfers passengers and goods between island corridors.</p>"},
      {id:"integration",title:"Capital of the whole island",html:"<p>Japanese acquisition of northern Sakhalin enlarges the capital's jurisdiction. Ministries and firms add northern oil, coal, fisheries, indigenous affairs, Russian-language records, and security coordination to institutions designed for the southern prefecture.</p>"},
      {id:"society",title:"A regional Japanese city",html:"<p>Japanese is the language of government, education, and advancement. Korean, Russian, Ainu, Nivkh, and Uilta residents enter the city as workers, students, patients, petitioners, and migrants. Formal imperial nationality coexists with strong residential and occupational hierarchy.</p>"},
      {id:"institutions",title:"Education, science, and northern administration",html:"<p>Technical schools, forestry and fisheries laboratories, weather services, hospitals, cultural archives, and civil-service training make Toyohara the island's knowledge center. Preservationist programs later collect languages and material culture while resource agencies continue to dominate budgets.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Toyohara in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Toyohara is a prosperous but administratively heavy northern capital. Its growth depends on state payrolls, rail and air links, resource headquarters, and military demand more than on an independent commercial hinterland. Tokyo treats it as the ordinary capital of Karafuto and the exceptional headquarters of a frontier.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Region",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"maoka.html",kicker:"Western port",label:"Maoka"},{href:"karafuto-resource-industries.html",kicker:"Economic base",label:"Karafuto Resource Industries"},{href:"northern-karafuto-integration.html",kicker:"Expanded jurisdiction",label:"Northern Karafuto Integration"},{href:"cities-of-imperial-japan.html",kicker:"Urban system",label:"Cities of Imperial Japan"}],
    facts:[["Historical Russian name","Vladimirovka"],["Japanese name","Toyohara"],["Capital since","1908"],["Region","Karafuto"],["Setting","Susuya Valley"],["Principal functions","Government, rail, education, services, and resource administration"],["Public language","Japanese"],["Status in 1985","Capital of all-island Karafuto"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk",label:"Historical and geographical reference — Toyohara / Yuzhno-Sakhalinsk"}]
  }),

  "maoka":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Maoka",category:"Cities of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Western port of Karafuto",
    lead:"Maoka is the principal port on Karafuto's west coast and the island's major gateway to the Sea of Japan. Its harbor, railway connection to Toyohara, fisheries, timber traffic, ship repair, and coastal shipping make it a practical counterpart to the inland capital and to the Okhotsk-facing ports of the east.",
    canon:"Maoka's Japanese name, west-coast location, historical port development, rail connection, fishing, forestry, and industrial role are historical. Its uninterrupted Japanese development after 1945 and mature place in the all-island transport system are extrapolated; harbor dimensions, population, fleet lists, and municipal politics remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"harbor",title:"West-coast harbor",html:"<p>Maoka faces the Sea of Japan and provides a shorter maritime approach from Hokkaidō and the western home-island network than the Okhotsk ports. Weather, ice, exposed approaches, and limited sheltered water make dredging, breakwaters, pilotage, and storage persistent concerns.</p>"},
      {id:"japanese",title:"Japanese port development",html:"<p>After 1905 Japanese authorities expanded the harbor, municipal grid, railway, warehouses, fisheries, sawmills, and worker housing. The city became one of southern Karafuto's largest settlements and a principal entry point for people and machinery.</p>"},
      {id:"rail",title:"Maoka–Toyohara corridor",html:"<p>The railway across the southern mountains links Maoka with <a href='toyohara.html'>Toyohara</a>. Imported food, fuel, machinery, and consumer goods move inland; timber, pulp, coal, fish, and processed cargo move outward. Disruption on the route affects the whole prefectural economy.</p>"},
      {id:"industry",title:"Fisheries, forestry, and repair",html:"<p>Fish landing and processing, timber handling, paper-related freight, cold storage, small ship repair, and railway workshops dominate the working waterfront. The port supports seasonal labor and a large logistics population rather than existing solely as a naval base.</p>"},
      {id:"north",title:"All-island traffic after Soviet collapse",html:"<p>Integration of northern Sakhalin redirects some oil, coal, timber, and passenger traffic through Maoka while new northern ports develop. The city becomes one node in a larger network rather than the unquestioned terminal for every western shipment.</p>"},
      {id:"people",title:"Port society",html:"<p>Japanese officials, merchants, fishermen, railway workers, Korean labor communities, and migrants from across the island share the city unequally. Maritime work preserves contact with Hokkaidō while exposing residents to price swings, accidents, storms, and seasonal unemployment.</p>"},
      {id:"security",title:"Strategic and civil shipping",html:"<p>Naval patrol, customs, coastal defense, merchant traffic, fishing fleets, and emergency supply use the same approaches. Military authority can close or prioritize the harbor, but civilian firms and prefectural agencies operate most daily traffic.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Maoka in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Maoka remains Karafuto's western commercial gateway. Container handling, refrigerated cargo, mechanized timber loading, rail-ferry proposals or services, and modern weather systems coexist with older industrial districts. Exact postwar harbor projects remain open.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Region",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"toyohara.html",kicker:"Inland capital",label:"Toyohara"},{href:"karafuto-resource-industries.html",kicker:"Cargo system",label:"Karafuto Resource Industries"},{href:"sea-of-okhotsk-transport-system.html",kicker:"Northern maritime comparison",label:"Sea of Okhotsk Transport System"},{href:"japanese-merchant-marine.html",kicker:"National shipping",label:"Japanese Merchant Marine"}],
    facts:[["Russian counterpart name","Kholmsk"],["Japanese name","Maoka"],["Region","Western Karafuto"],["Connected capital","Toyohara"],["Principal transport","Port and railway"],["Principal cargoes","Fish, timber, pulp, coal, machinery, and provisions"],["Strategic water","Sea of Japan"],["Status in 1985","Principal western port of Karafuto"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kholmsk",label:"Historical and geographical reference — Maoka / Kholmsk"}]
  }),

  "northern-karafuto-integration":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Northern Karafuto integration",category:"Territorial history",infoboxKicker:"Incorporation of northern Sakhalin into Japan",
    lead:"Northern Karafuto integration is the military, administrative, economic, and nationality process by which Japan extended its southern Karafuto institutions across the former Soviet half of Sakhalin. It began as a security occupation during Soviet collapse and became the constitutional basis for treating the entire island as one region of Japan proper.",
    canon:"Japan's ownership of all Sakhalin and integral treatment of Karafuto are explicit canon. The historical 1920–25 occupation and prewar northern oil and coal concessions provide the baseline. The exact 1940s campaign dates, legal instrument, boundary administration, Russian settlement policy, and sequence of municipal renaming remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"precedent",title:"The 1920–1925 precedent",html:"<p>Japan occupied northern Sakhalin after the Nikolaevsk Incident and withdrew in 1925 when relations with the Soviet Union were normalized. The occupation produced surveys, routes, commercial knowledge, and a political memory of the north as recoverable Japanese strategic space.</p>"},
      {id:"concessions",title:"Oil and coal concessions",html:"<p>The Soviet–Japanese Basic Convention permitted Japanese petroleum and coal concessions in northern Sakhalin. Firms and engineers gained practical familiarity with fields, ports, labor, climate, and transport even though Soviet sovereignty continued.</p>"},
      {id:"collapse",title:"Soviet collapse",html:"<p>As Soviet authority east of Lake Baikal fragmented, Japanese forces secured the straits, northern ports, fuel installations, wireless stations, and airfields. Local Soviet offices, enterprise managers, workers, security units, and indigenous communities faced an occupying authority that intended permanent control rather than temporary protection.</p>"},
      {id:"administration",title:"Extension of Karafuto government",html:"<p>The Karafuto Agency and later prefectural institutions extend cadastral, police, court, school, health, transport, and taxation systems northward. Military and corporate offices remain unusually powerful because civil administration depends on their shipping, fuel, records, and security.</p>"},
      {id:"nationality",title:"Nationality and inherited residents",html:"<p>All-island Karafuto becomes integral Japan. Japanese settlers arrive, while Russians, Nivkh, Uilta, Evenks, Koreans, and mixed families are registered through transitional categories before incorporation into regional Japanese nationality law. Exact options for departure, retention, and naturalization remain open.</p>"},
      {id:"names",title:"Names and public geography",html:"<p>Karafuto becomes the official name for the whole island. Older Japanese forms, Russian city names, indigenous place-names, and corporate designations coexist in northern usage. The archive leaves the final official name of several northern towns open rather than silently inventing a complete renaming program.</p>"},
      {id:"infrastructure",title:"Joining two island economies",html:"<p>Rail extension, roads, ports, pipelines, aviation, weather services, and coastal shipping join northern oil and coal districts to the more developed south. The integration remains incomplete: the island is one jurisdiction, but seasonal transport and company systems preserve distinct northern zones.</p>"},
      {id:"memory",title:"Political memory",html:"<p>Official history describes reunification of a Japanese island and rescue from Soviet collapse. Russian and indigenous memory describes occupation, registry change, settlement pressure, and loss of political choice. Preservation-era museums acknowledge older populations while retaining the territorial settlement.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Territorial outcome",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"karafuto-resource-industries.html",kicker:"Economic motive",label:"Karafuto Resource Industries"},{href:"russian-far-eastern-state.html",kicker:"Continental neighbor",label:"Russian Far Eastern State"},{href:"soviet-collapse.html",kicker:"Founding crisis",label:"Soviet Collapse"},{href:"nivkh-people.html",kicker:"Cross-border people",label:"Nivkh People"},{href:"uilta-people.html",kicker:"Sakhalin people",label:"Uilta People"}],
    facts:[["Territory integrated","Northern Sakhalin"],["Historical precedent","Japanese occupation, 1920–1925"],["Prewar presence","Oil and coal concessions"],["Immediate cause","Collapse of Soviet authority"],["Administering region","Karafuto"],["Constitutional outcome","Integral Japan"],["Official island name","Karafuto"],["Exact legal instrument","Open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karafuto_Prefecture",label:"Historical reference — Japanese occupation and northern concessions"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakhalin",label:"Geographical reference — Sakhalin"}]
  }),

  "karafuto-resource-industries":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Resource industries of Karafuto",category:"Economic history",infoboxKicker:"Forestry, fisheries, coal, oil, and northern logistics",
    lead:"Karafuto's resource industries are the forestry, pulp and paper, fisheries, coal, petroleum, natural-gas, agricultural, transport, and cold-region engineering systems through which Sakhalin serves the Japanese economy. They make the island materially important while binding its settlements to corporate concessions, seasonal labor, and strategic shipping.",
    canon:"The historical southern economy of fishing, forestry, agriculture, coal, petroleum, paper, charcoal, railway construction, labor shortage, and Japanese northern concessions is baseline. Whole-island control and integration into the mature imperial resource network are canon or direct extrapolation. Production totals, firms, pipelines, reserves, wages, and postwar project dates remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"forest",title:"Forestry, pulp, and paper",html:"<p>Conifer forests supply saw timber, pulp, paper, charcoal, and cellulose inputs. Rail spurs, river transport, winter roads, camps, mills, and ports connect cutting districts to Japanese factories. Replanting and fire control lag behind extraction in some concession zones.</p>"},
      {id:"fish",title:"Fisheries and marine processing",html:"<p>Salmon, herring, cod, crab, and other cold-water fisheries support canneries, freezing plants, fishmeal, coastal fleets, and seasonal employment. Japanese maritime regulation and capital dominate access, while indigenous and local fishing knowledge remains operationally important.</p>"},
      {id:"coal",title:"Coal and industrial fuel",html:"<p>Coal mines supply local rail, power, heat, shipping, and industry and feed the wider home-island market. Mechanization, ventilation, winter transport, accidents, and labor recruitment determine whether nominal reserves become dependable output.</p>"},
      {id:"oil",title:"Northern oil and gas",html:"<p>Japanese companies knew the northern fields through prewar concessions. Permanent control permits larger drilling, pipelines, storage, refineries or terminals, and geophysical work. Karafuto petroleum remains valuable because it lies inside Japan proper, even when tropical and Middle Eastern imports are cheaper.</p>"},
      {id:"labor",title:"Labor and settlement",html:"<p>The prewar territory relied on mainland seasonal workers, Korean labor, bonded contracts, and indigenous participation under unequal terms. Postwar policy combines Japanese settlement, corporate housing, mechanization, technical schools, and continuing migrant labor. Labor shortage encourages automation earlier than humanitarian reform would have done.</p>"},
      {id:"transport",title:"Railways, ports, and pipelines",html:"<p>The network joins <a href='toyohara.html'>Toyohara</a>, <a href='maoka.html'>Maoka</a>, southern ports, forest and coal districts, and northern oil fields. Breaks in gauge, loading practice, maintenance, or weather systems inherited from the divided island require long standardization.</p>"},
      {id:"state",title:"State and corporate direction",html:"<p>Prefectural agencies allocate land and permits; national-policy institutions finance strategic projects; trading companies arrange machinery, shipping, insurance, and sales; operating firms manage sites. This is a Japanese resource network rather than a free regional commodity market.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"The mature system",html:"<p>By 1985 Karafuto supplies strategic fuel, paper inputs, fish, timber, and northern technical services. Resource prosperity remains geographically narrow and environmentally costly. Toyohara's administrators and local councils bargain for infrastructure and revenue while Tokyo protects continuous supply.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Regional setting",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"toyohara.html",kicker:"Administrative center",label:"Toyohara"},{href:"maoka.html",kicker:"Export port",label:"Maoka"},{href:"northern-karafuto-integration.html",kicker:"Territorial expansion",label:"Northern Karafuto Integration"},{href:"japanese-imperial-economic-system.html",kicker:"Imperial framework",label:"Co-Prosperity Economic System"},{href:"sogo-shosha.html",kicker:"Commercial network",label:"Sōgō Shōsha"}],
    facts:[["Principal sectors","Forestry, pulp and paper, fisheries, coal, oil, gas, and transport"],["Strategic advantage","Resources inside Japan proper"],["Prewar northern access","Japanese oil and coal concessions"],["Principal administrative center","Toyohara"],["Principal western port","Maoka"],["Persistent constraint","Labor and seasonal transport"],["Institutional form","Prefectural regulation and corporate operation"],["Status in 1985","Mature strategic resource region"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karafuto_Prefecture",label:"Historical economic reference — Karafuto Prefecture"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakhalin",label:"Geographical and resource reference — Sakhalin"}]
  }),

  "ainu-people":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ainu people",category:"Peoples of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Indigenous people of Hokkaidō, Karafuto, and the Kurils",
    lead:"The Ainu are an indigenous people of northern Japan and the Okhotsk world, historically centered in Hokkaidō, Karafuto, and the Kuril Islands. Japanese settlement, land law, schooling, labor regulation, and cultural suppression produced deep assimilation before the divergence; victory preserves those institutions and later makes Ainu recognition a test of imperial preservationism.",
    canon:"Ainu geography, colonial incorporation, forced assimilation, language suppression, subsistence traditions, and cultural history through 1941 are historical. Continued Japanese rule, absence of American occupation reform, all-island Karafuto, and the later preservationist setting are fixed. Exact population, legal recognition date, organizations, elected representatives, and cultural budgets remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"homeland",title:"Northern homeland",html:"<p>Ainu communities historically occupied Hokkaidō, southern and central Karafuto, the Kurils, and parts of the Okhotsk rim. Fishing, hunting, gathering, trade, river and maritime travel, and relations with Japanese, Nivkh, Uilta, and other northern peoples varied by locality.</p>"},
      {id:"japan",title:"Japanese expansion and assimilation",html:"<p>Matsumae trade control, Meiji annexation, settler agriculture, fisheries regulation, land seizure, compulsory schooling, and restrictions on subsistence transformed Ainu life. Official policy classified the population as former aborigines while demanding Japanese language, names, occupations, and public custom.</p>"},
      {id:"karafuto",title:"Sakhalin Ainu",html:"<p>Karafuto Ainu maintained distinct communities and speech from Hokkaidō Ainu. Japanese administration counted and resettled communities, recruited labor, and placed schooling and welfare under colonial institutions. Because no Soviet conquest removes the Japanese population, Sakhalin Ainu remain inside <a href='karafuto.html'>Karafuto</a> rather than undergoing the historical postwar repatriation.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"Culture and religion",html:"<p>Kinship, oral literature, carved prayer sticks, bear ceremony, spirits or <em>kamuy</em>, textiles, woodwork, fishing, hunting, and foodways form a living culture rather than a museum list. Christian, Buddhist, Shinto, and secular influences enter unevenly without producing one uniform Ainu religious identity.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Language loss",html:"<p><a href='ainu-language.html'>Ainu languages</a> retreat under Japanese-only education, mixed settlement, wage work, and stigma. Hokkaidō, Sakhalin, and Kuril forms do not remain equally viable. By 1985 fluent intergenerational speech is rare and preservation depends on elderly speakers, recordings, classes, and families willing to reverse language shift.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"Victory without occupation reform",html:"<p>Japan's avoidance of war with the United States means no Allied occupation abolishes or rewrites northern assimilation policy. Wartime mobilization, resource extraction, and settlement continue into the victory order, giving later reformers fewer legal breaks on which to build.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preservationist recognition",html:"<p>The rise of <a href='japanese-preservationism.html'>Japanese preservationism</a> in the 1970s supports archives, cultural centers, language work, ceremonies, land-history research, and limited consultative bodies. The state prefers heritage recognition compatible with imperial unity and resists claims that imply separate sovereignty.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Ainu society in 1985",html:"<p>Ainu identity survives in Hokkaidō, Karafuto, urban families, cultural associations, and personal ancestry often concealed from official records. Recognition is more visible than power. Questions of land, fishing access, discrimination, remains, language, and who may speak for dispersed communities remain unsettled.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ainu-language.html",kicker:"Heritage language",label:"Ainu Language"},{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Island homeland",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"nivkh-people.html",kicker:"Okhotsk neighbor",label:"Nivkh People"},{href:"uilta-people.html",kicker:"Sakhalin neighbor",label:"Uilta People"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Policy context",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Imperial law",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"}],
    facts:[["Self-designation","Ainu · human being"],["Principal homelands","Hokkaidō, Karafuto, and the Kurils"],["Language","Ainu languages; extensive Japanese shift"],["State nationality","Japanese"],["Historical economy","Fishing, hunting, gathering, trade, and craft"],["Principal historical pressure","Japanese settlement and forced assimilation"],["Postwar external occupation","None"],["Status in 1985","Recognized indigenous minority with severely weakened language"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_people",label:"Historical and ethnographic reference — Ainu people"}]
  }),

  "ainu-language":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ainu language",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Endangered language family of northern Japan",
    lead:"Ainu is the name used for the closely related indigenous language varieties historically spoken in Hokkaidō, Karafuto, and the Kuril Islands. It is unrelated to Japanese in demonstrated classification and has no secure wider family affiliation. By 1985 it is critically endangered after generations of Japanese-language schooling, settlement, stigma, and disrupted community transmission.",
    canon:"The language's classification, regional varieties, oral tradition, lack of a traditional indigenous script, Japanese transcription, and severe historical decline are baseline. Continued assimilation and later preservation programs follow setting canon. Exact speaker counts, vitality by district, orthographic authority, and which Sakhalin or Kuril varieties retain fluent speakers in 1985 remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"classification",title:"Classification",html:"<p>Ainu is conventionally treated as a small language family or isolate without a demonstrated genetic relationship to Japanese. Hokkaidō, Sakhalin, and Kuril varieties differ substantially enough that a single standardized classroom form cannot represent every historical community.</p>"},
      {id:"structure",title:"Linguistic structure",html:"<p>Ainu uses rich verbal morphology, person marking, derivation, and grammatical patterns distinct from Japanese. Place-names across Hokkaidō and Karafuto preserve Ainu geography even where later speakers no longer understand the original forms.</p>"},
      {id:"oral",title:"Oral literature",html:"<p>Narrative epics, songs, prayers, stories, place knowledge, ritual speech, and personal testimony form the language's principal historical archive. Performance and memorized formula carry social information that word lists alone cannot preserve.</p>"},
      {id:"writing",title:"Writing and transcription",html:"<p>Ainu had no single traditional indigenous script. Japanese kana, Latin transcription, linguistic notation, and locally adapted teaching systems record it. Competing orthographies reflect whether a project prioritizes scholarship, community pronunciation, school accessibility, or historical documentation.</p>"},
      {id:"suppression",title:"Japanese-language shift",html:"<p>Compulsory education, land and labor integration, intermarriage, official stigma, military service, and urban migration move families toward Japanese. Children learn that Ainu limits advancement and that Japanese provides access to every public institution.</p>"},
      {id:"regional",title:"Hokkaidō, Karafuto, and Kuril varieties",html:"<p>Hokkaidō contains the largest documentation base. Karafuto Ainu develops in contact with Nivkh, Uilta, Russian, and Japanese. Kuril communities suffer resettlement and dispersal. The three regional histories require separate archival and revival work.</p>"},
      {id:"revival",title:"Preservation and teaching",html:"<p>Preservationist institutions fund recordings, dictionaries, place-name studies, adult classes, cultural centers, broadcasts, and school materials. A policy may create competent learners and public visibility without restoring a household language or a community of native-speaking children.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>Ainu is critically endangered across its historical range. The archive does not assign a precise count because later real-world figures cannot be transferred unchanged into a Japan without defeat, occupation, or repatriation. Viability differs sharply between elderly speakers, revival learners, and archival varieties.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ainu-people.html",kicker:"Speakers",label:"Ainu People"},{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Northern territory",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Imperial comparison",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"authenticity-language-preservation-japan.html",kicker:"Revival debate",label:"Authenticity in Language Preservation"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Political movement",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Family","Unclassified; commonly treated as an isolate or small family"],["Historical varieties","Hokkaidō, Sakhalin, and Kuril Ainu"],["Traditional transmission","Oral"],["Writing","Japanese kana and Latin-based transcriptions"],["Principal contact language","Japanese"],["Major preserved material","Epic, prayer, song, narrative, and place-name traditions"],["Public support","Archives, classes, broadcasting, and cultural centers"],["1985 status","Critically endangered; exact speaker count open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ainu_language",label:"Linguistic reference — Ainu language"}]
  }),

  "nivkh-people":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Nivkh people",category:"Indigenous peoples of Northeast Asia",infoboxKicker:"Lower-Amur and Karafuto fishing people",
    lead:"The Nivkh are an indigenous people of the lower Amur, the Strait of Tartary, and northern Karafuto. Their river and maritime economy, dog breeding, fishing, language, and cross-strait kinship predate both Russian and Japanese rule. In the divided post-Soviet order, Nivkh communities live on both sides of a heavily supervised Japanese–Far Eastern frontier.",
    canon:"Nivkh geography, fishing economy, language distinctiveness, Russian and Japanese colonial contact, and northern Sakhalin presence are historical. Japan's all-island Karafuto and the protected Russian Far Eastern State establish the setting's cross-border division. Exact population, districts, councils, schooling, border visits, and language status in 1985 remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"homeland",title:"Lower Amur and Sakhalin homeland",html:"<p>Nivkh communities occupy the lower Amur basin, estuary, mainland coast, and northern Sakhalin. Rivers, sea ice, salmon runs, coastal waters, forest resources, and seasonal movement connect places later divided by international boundaries.</p>"},
      {id:"subsistence",title:"Fishing, transport, and dogs",html:"<p>Salmon fishing has historically supplied food, trade, storage, and ritual life. Hunting, gathering, maritime work, and dog transport supplement it. Industrial fisheries, river regulation, settlement concentration, and company access can damage the same ecological system on which local autonomy depends.</p>"},
      {id:"empires",title:"Qing, Russian, and Japanese frontiers",html:"<p>Nivkh communities dealt with tribute, traders, missions, settlers, administrators, and military posts from several empires. Neither Russian nor Japanese rule encountered an empty frontier; both translated existing kinship and exchange routes into ethnic categories and administrative districts.</p>"},
      {id:"karafuto",title:"Nivkh under Karafuto administration",html:"<p>Japanese acquisition of northern Sakhalin places the island population inside <a href='karafuto.html'>Karafuto</a>. Fisheries, schools, police, medical services, settlement policy, and corporate employment extend into Nivkh districts. Japanese nationality is formal; cultural and administrative equality is not.</p>"},
      {id:"amur",title:"Communities in the Russian Far Eastern State",html:"<p>Mainland communities remain within the Russian Far Eastern State. Local councils, Russian administration, transport subsidies, and <a href='indigenous-policy-russian-far-eastern-state.html'>federal indigenous policy</a> differ from the Japanese system while sharing dependence on distant capitals and commercial extraction.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Nivkh language",html:"<p>Nivkh is a small, internally varied language family or isolate with no demonstrated close relative. Russian and Japanese dominate their respective public systems. Cross-border division complicates orthography, schooling, publishing, recordings, and ordinary family contact.</p>"},
      {id:"border",title:"The supervised strait",html:"<p>Fishing, rescue, weather, scientific work, regulated visits, and family petitions cross the strait under Japanese and Far Eastern scrutiny. The border protects strategic approaches and corporate concessions while cutting across an older Nivkh geography.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Nivkh society in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Nivkh identity survives in villages, fishing enterprises, urban households, councils, museums, and a fragile language community. Officials on both sides claim protective policy. Resource pressure, schooling, migration, and restricted movement determine whether that recognition produces living institutions.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Island jurisdiction",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"northern-karafuto-integration.html",kicker:"Territorial change",label:"Northern Karafuto Integration"},{href:"ainu-people.html",kicker:"Southern neighbor",label:"Ainu People"},{href:"uilta-people.html",kicker:"Sakhalin neighbor",label:"Uilta People"},{href:"russian-far-eastern-state.html",kicker:"Mainland state",label:"Russian Far Eastern State"},{href:"amur-settlement.html",kicker:"Border order",label:"Amur Settlement"}],
    facts:[["Self-designation","Nivkh; regional forms vary"],["Historical Russian exonym","Gilyak"],["Homeland","Lower Amur, Strait of Tartary, and northern Karafuto"],["Language","Nivkh"],["Language classification","Small family or isolate"],["Historical economic center","Salmon fishing"],["1985 jurisdictions","Japan and the Russian Far Eastern State"],["Principal political problem","A cross-border people divided by strategic administration"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nivkh_people",label:"Historical and ethnographic reference — Nivkh people"}]
  }),

  "uilta-people":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Uilta people",category:"Indigenous peoples of Northeast Asia",infoboxKicker:"Tungusic reindeer people of Karafuto",
    lead:"The Uilta, historically called Oroks in Russian and Japanese records, are a small Tungusic people of Sakhalin whose pastoral, hunting, fishing, and transport traditions are associated especially with reindeer. Japan's retention and expansion of Karafuto keeps their principal homeland inside the empire rather than subjecting the community to the historical Soviet conquest and postwar displacement.",
    canon:"The Uilta/Orok names, Tungusic language, Sakhalin homeland, reindeer use, Japanese colonial contact, and historical community size are baseline. Continued residence under all-island Japanese Karafuto is a direct setting consequence. Exact population, villages, legal name, school policy, councils, and language vitality remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"names",title:"Names",html:"<p>Uilta is the preferred self-designation in modern ethnographic usage. Orok, Orochen, and related forms appear in Russian and Japanese records and can be confused with other Tungusic peoples. An in-setting archive therefore preserves historical labels while identifying the people as Uilta.</p>"},
      {id:"homeland",title:"Sakhalin homeland",html:"<p>Uilta communities historically lived in northern and central Sakhalin, moving through tundra, forest, river, and coastal zones. Their geography overlaps Nivkh, Evenk, Ainu, Russian, and Japanese worlds without reducing them to any one neighboring population.</p>"},
      {id:"economy",title:"Reindeer, hunting, and fishing",html:"<p>Reindeer provide transport, livelihood, and a means of moving between seasonal grounds. Hunting, fishing, gathering, trade, and later wage labor supplement pastoral life. Roads, pipelines, forestry, and oil fields disrupt routes even when companies hire local guides and drivers.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Uilta language",html:"<p>Uilta belongs to the southern Tungusic languages and is related to Nanai and Ulch. Japanese and Russian contact shape vocabulary and bilingualism. Small population and dispersed households make intergenerational transmission highly vulnerable.</p>"},
      {id:"japanese",title:"Japanese Karafuto",html:"<p>Karafuto administration registers communities, regulates movement and hunting, supplies schools and clinics, and recruits indigenous labor. The relationship combines coercive settlement policy, paternal welfare, ethnographic interest, and practical dependence on local knowledge.</p>"},
      {id:"integration",title:"After northern integration",html:"<p><a href='northern-karafuto-integration.html'>Northern integration</a> brings most Uilta communities under one Japanese island government. This removes an international boundary within Sakhalin while placing land, herds, and routes under a larger resource and security administration.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preservation and representation",html:"<p>Late preservation policy supports recordings, dictionaries, craft work, festivals, museum collections, and consultative representation. Because the community is small, a cultural program may become dependent on a few families and officials and may preserve display more readily than daily speech.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Uilta society in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 Uilta people are Japanese nationals of Karafuto with a recognized regional identity and little independent political weight. Japanese dominates public life. Language and reindeer knowledge survive unevenly, while company development makes land consultation the central practical question.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"karafuto.html",kicker:"Homeland",label:"Karafuto"},{href:"northern-karafuto-integration.html",kicker:"Political history",label:"Northern Karafuto Integration"},{href:"nivkh-people.html",kicker:"Northern neighbor",label:"Nivkh People"},{href:"ainu-people.html",kicker:"Southern neighbor",label:"Ainu People"},{href:"evenks.html",kicker:"Tungusic comparison",label:"Evenks"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural policy",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Preferred name","Uilta"],["Historical exonym","Orok"],["Homeland","Northern and central Karafuto"],["Language","Uilta"],["Language family","Tungusic"],["Characteristic livelihood","Reindeer pastoralism with hunting and fishing"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"],["1985 condition","Small recognized community under severe language pressure"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orok_people",label:"Historical and ethnographic reference — Uilta / Orok people"}]
  }),

  "ryukyu-islands":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ryukyu Islands",infoboxKicker:"Southwestern island region of Japan proper",
    portrait:{src:"assets/flags/japan.svg",alt:"Flag of the Empire of Japan",caption:"The Ryukyu Islands form part of Japan proper and use the Japanese national flag"},
    lead:"The Ryukyu Islands are the southwestern island chain extending from Kyūshū toward Taiwan. Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama, Amami, and neighboring groups form a strategic and cultural corridor between the home islands, Formosa, the East China Sea, and the Philippine approaches. Japan's limited-war strategy prevents an American invasion and occupation, leaving the islands under uninterrupted imperial rule after 1941.",
    canon:"The geography, island groups, Ryukyu Kingdom, Satsuma domination, 1879 annexation, Okinawa Prefecture, and pre-1941 assimilation are historical. No Pacific war with America, no Battle of Okinawa, uninterrupted Japanese sovereignty, integral status, settlement links to Taiwan, and later preservation politics follow established setting canon. Population, bases, postwar prefectural politics, and exact language statistics remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"geography",title:"The Nansei chain",html:"<p>The Nansei or Southwest Islands stretch from Kyūshū to Taiwan between the East China Sea and Philippine Sea. Administrative and cultural definitions differ: northern groups belong to Kagoshima Prefecture, while Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama, Daitō, and related islands form Okinawa Prefecture.</p>"},
      {id:"kingdom",title:"Ryukyu Kingdom",html:"<p>The Ryukyu Kingdom united much of the island world from the fifteenth century, traded through tributary and maritime networks, and developed a court centered at Shuri. Satsuma invaded in 1609 and imposed overlordship while preserving the kingdom's useful external forms.</p>"},
      {id:"annexation",title:"Annexation and prefectural rule",html:"<p>The Meiji state abolished the kingdom and created Okinawa Prefecture in 1879. Japanese schooling, conscription, land reform, police, administration, and discrimination brought the islands into the national state while treating Ryukyuan speech and custom as backward.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"A Pacific war without Okinawa",html:"<p>Japan excludes American territory from the southern war and the United States never enters the conflict. There is no American island campaign, Battle of Okinawa, occupation government, base seizure, or postwar reversion. The islands experience mobilization and austerity but avoid the historical destruction and demographic rupture of 1945.</p>"},
      {id:"strategy",title:"Imperial strategic corridor",html:"<p>Airfields, naval anchorages, radio, weather, shipping, and surveillance make the chain a fortified approach to Japan and a bridge toward Taiwan. Strategic importance strengthens Tokyo's direct authority even where the ordinary legal form remains prefectural.</p>"},
      {id:"taiwan",title:"Migration to Taiwan and the South Seas",html:"<p>Ryukyuans join the postwar settlement of <a href='taiwan.html'>Taiwan</a> as farmers, fishermen, workers, technicians, and families. Others move through the directly ruled Pacific. Their Japanese nationality enables movement while regional origin continues to shape class, accent, and networks.</p>"},
      {id:"culture",title:"Language and regional culture",html:"<p><a href='ryukyuan-people.html'>Ryukyuan identity</a>, local religion, music, dance, textiles, food, court traditions, island kinship, and <a href='ryukyuan-languages.html'>Japonic languages</a> survive under strong Japanese assimilation. Cultural continuity differs greatly among Amami, Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama, and Yonaguni.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"The islands in 1985",html:"<p>By 1985 the Ryukyus are integral and strategically dense Japanese islands with a visible regional identity. Japanese is universal in public life and increasingly dominant at home. Preservation programs support local languages and heritage, but no major state movement has displaced the assumption of Japanese sovereignty.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyuan-people.html",kicker:"Regional people",label:"Ryukyuan People"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Southern continuation",label:"Taiwan"},{href:"integral-territories-japan.html",kicker:"Constitutional status",label:"Integral Territories of Japan"},{href:"japan.html",kicker:"Sovereign state",label:"Empire of Japan"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural politics",label:"Japanese Preservationism"}],
    facts:[["Japanese geographic name","Nansei-shotō"],["Principal prefectures","Kagoshima and Okinawa"],["Largest island","Okinawa Island"],["Historical state","Ryukyu Kingdom"],["Japanese annexation","1879"],["American wartime invasion","None"],["Nationality","Japanese"],["Status in 1985","Integral southwestern region of Japan proper"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyu_Islands",label:"Geographical and historical reference — Ryukyu Islands"},{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Ryukyu_Islands",label:"Historical reference — Ryukyu Islands"}]
  }),

  "ryukyuan-people":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ryukyuan people",category:"Peoples of Imperial Japan",infoboxKicker:"Japonic people of the southwestern islands",
    lead:"The Ryukyuan people are the indigenous regional populations of Amami, Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama, Yonaguni, and related southwestern islands. They share historical connections to the Ryukyu Kingdom and Japonic languages while retaining strong island and subregional identities; many residents identify by island or prefecture as readily as by a single Ryukyuan category.",
    canon:"Ryukyuan geography, kingdom history, Japonic relationship, annexation, assimilation, migration, and internal diversity are historical. Uninterrupted Japanese rule, absence of American occupation, settlement in Taiwan, integral nationality, and preservationist politics follow setting canon. Exact census identity, organizations, party alignments, and recognition law remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"identity",title:"Identity and internal diversity",html:"<p>Ryukyuan is a useful collective term, but Amami, Okinawan, Miyakoan, Yaeyama, Yonaguni, and smaller island identities possess their own histories. The old kingdom did not erase those differences, and modern prefectural government does not create one uniform ethnic community.</p>"},
      {id:"kingdom",title:"Kingdom and maritime culture",html:"<p>The Ryukyu Kingdom connected Japan, China, Korea, Southeast Asia, and the island world through diplomacy and trade. Court ceremony, religion, music, dance, navigation, tribute, and local hierarchy developed under both Chinese recognition and Satsuma control.</p>"},
      {id:"annexation",title:"Japanese annexation and assimilation",html:"<p>After 1879, schools and officials promoted standard Japanese, imperial history, conscription, and prefectural institutions. Dialect punishment, occupational discrimination, poverty, and migration encouraged families to suppress regional speech and present themselves as ordinary Japanese.</p>"},
      {id:"war",title:"Mobilization without invasion",html:"<p>The southern war brings military construction, requisition, service, and surveillance but no American assault or occupation. Avoidance of the Battle of Okinawa saves towns and families from historical mass destruction while leaving prewar imperial institutions intact.</p>"},
      {id:"migration",title:"Migration and imperial settlement",html:"<p>Economic pressure already sent Ryukyuans to the home islands and overseas. Victory creates new routes to Taiwan, the South Seas, shipping, fisheries, military service, and technical work. Migrants possess Japanese nationality but often occupy a socially marked regional position.</p>"},
      {id:"language",title:"Language shift",html:"<p>The <a href='ryukyuan-languages.html'>Ryukyuan languages</a> lose children to Japanese through school, employment, media, military service, and mixed migration. Okinawan Japanese and regional accents persist even as distinct languages retreat.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preservation and heritage",html:"<p>From the 1970s, scholars, performers, teachers, religious custodians, broadcasters, and local associations gain support for archives, festivals, classes, place-names, court arts, and island history. Tokyo accepts culture more readily than claims of separate nationhood.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Ryukyuan society in 1985",html:"<p>Ryukyuans are Japanese citizens and a large, internally diverse regional people. Their island institutions and diaspora networks remain durable. Public Japanese identity can coexist with Ryukyuan ancestry, religious practice, music, family memory, and political resentment of military concentration.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyu-islands.html",kicker:"Homeland",label:"Ryukyu Islands"},{href:"ryukyuan-languages.html",kicker:"Language family",label:"Ryukyuan Languages"},{href:"taiwan.html",kicker:"Migration destination",label:"Taiwan"},{href:"japanese-settlement-of-taiwan.html",kicker:"Postwar diaspora",label:"Japanese Settlement of Taiwan"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Cultural politics",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"ainu-people.html",kicker:"Northern comparison",label:"Ainu People"}],
    facts:[["Collective name","Ryukyuan people"],["Principal regions","Amami, Okinawa, Miyako, Yaeyama, and Yonaguni"],["Language family","Japonic"],["Historical state","Ryukyu Kingdom"],["Japanese annexation","1879"],["Nationality in 1985","Japanese"],["Major postwar migration","Taiwan and the Japanese Pacific"],["1985 position","Recognized regional identity within integral Japan"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyuan_people",label:"Historical and ethnographic reference — Ryukyuan people"}]
  }),

  "ryukyuan-languages":karafutoRyukyuArticle({
    title:"Ryukyuan languages",category:"Languages",infoboxKicker:"Endangered Japonic languages of the southwestern islands",
    lead:"The Ryukyuan languages are the Japonic languages of Amami, Okinawa, Kunigami, Miyako, Yaeyama, Yonaguni, and related island communities. They are relatives of Japanese rather than merely corrupted forms of standard Japanese, although imperial schools and much public usage classify them as dialects. By 1985 every major branch is under pressure from Japanese-language shift.",
    canon:"Japonic classification, principal branches, historical suppression, dialect labeling, Japanese shift, and language distinctiveness are baseline. Uninterrupted imperial rule, lack of American occupation, Taiwan migration, and preservationist intervention follow setting canon. Exact 1985 endangerment labels, speaker counts, school hours, orthographies, and broadcast schedules remain open.",
    sections:[
      {id:"family",title:"The southern Japonic family",html:"<p>Ryukyuan and Japanese descend from a shared Japonic ancestor. Internal divisions include northern languages in Amami and Okinawa and southern languages in Miyako, Yaeyama, and Yonaguni. Mutual comprehension is limited across several branches and with standard Japanese.</p>"},
      {id:"languages",title:"Principal languages",html:"<p>Amami, Kunigami, Okinawan, Miyako, Yaeyama, and Yonaguni are the principal named languages. Each contains island and village varieties. The label “Ryukyuan language” is therefore a family shorthand rather than one common spoken standard.</p>"},
      {id:"kingdom",title:"Speech in the kingdom",html:"<p>Local speech coexisted with court registers, classical Chinese writing, diplomatic languages, and forms shaped by the Shuri court. Political unity never required one vernacular language across the archipelago.</p>"},
      {id:"school",title:"Schooling and dialect suppression",html:"<p>Meiji and later schools teach standard Japanese and punish or shame local speech. The dialect-label system makes language replacement appear to be correction of provincial Japanese rather than loss of distinct languages.</p>"},
      {id:"postwar",title:"No occupation intermission",html:"<p>Because the United States never invades or governs Okinawa, Japanese schooling and broadcasting continue without an occupation-era institutional break. This continuity strengthens public integration and removes one possible space in which local political identity might have reorganized separately.</p>"},
      {id:"diaspora",title:"Taiwan and Pacific diaspora",html:"<p>Ryukyuan migrants carry island speech to Taiwan, the home islands, and Pacific territories. Migration may sustain adult networks for a generation, but children usually adopt Japanese because it serves school, employment, military, and interregional life.</p>"},
      {id:"preservation",title:"Preservation and authenticity",html:"<p>Programs record elderly speakers, teach songs and greetings, support local broadcasts, document place-names, and create orthographies. Advocates disagree over archival fidelity, a community standard, and a teachable living language. A single Okinawan-centered standard may marginalize smaller branches.</p>"},
      {id:"1985",title:"Status in 1985",html:"<p>All principal Ryukyuan languages are endangered, with the smallest southern varieties under the greatest pressure. Exact labels are left open because settlement and uninterrupted Japanese rule alter real-world trajectories. Okinawan Japanese remains vigorous but is not a substitute for the older languages.</p>"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"ryukyuan-people.html",kicker:"Speakers",label:"Ryukyuan People"},{href:"ryukyu-islands.html",kicker:"Homeland",label:"Ryukyu Islands"},{href:"authenticity-language-preservation-japan.html",kicker:"Standardization debate",label:"Authenticity in Language Preservation"},{href:"japanese-preservationism.html",kicker:"Policy movement",label:"Japanese Preservationism"},{href:"languages-of-taiwan.html",kicker:"Diaspora context",label:"Languages of Taiwan"},{href:"ainu-language.html",kicker:"Northern comparison",label:"Ainu Language"}],
    facts:[["Family","Japonic"],["Principal branches","Northern and Southern Ryukyuan"],["Named languages","Amami, Kunigami, Okinawan, Miyako, Yaeyama, and Yonaguni"],["Principal public contact language","Japanese"],["Traditional official classification","Regional dialects of Japanese"],["Writing","Japanese scripts and modern local orthographies"],["Wartime occupation break","None"],["1985 status","All major languages endangered; exact classifications open"]],
    sources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryukyuan_languages",label:"Linguistic reference — Ryukyuan languages"}]
  })
});

const addKarafutoRyukyuRelated = (slug, links) => {
  const article = window.deepArticles[slug];
  if (!article) return;
  article.related = article.related || [];
  for (const link of links) {
    if (!article.related.some(existing => existing.href === link.href)) article.related.push(link);
  }
};

